  "Number": 1,
  "Question": "The expression \\(\\dfrac{1^{4y-1}}{5^{-1}+3^{-1}}\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{4y-1}{8}\\)",
    "B": "\\(8\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{15}{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{15}{8}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac18\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Every real power of \\(1\\) equals \\(1\\), so the numerator does not depend on \\(y\\). Raising a nonzero number to the exponent \\(-1\\) gives its reciprocal. Therefore the denominator is\n\\[\n5^{-1}+3^{-1}\n=\\frac15+\\frac13\n=\\frac3{15}+\\frac5{15}\n=\\frac8{15}.\n\\]\nThis denominator is nonzero. Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal, so\n\\[\n\\frac{1^{4y-1}}{5^{-1}+3^{-1}}\n=1\\div\\frac8{15}\n=\\frac{15}{8}.\n\\]\nThe value is \\(\\boxed{15/8}\\), choice D.",
